\]
and we know that $H_1(\tau_u)=\operatorname{id}$, OK. That being so, let $I=\widehat Y\setminus Y$, $J=\widehat Y\setminus U$, in such a way that $J\supset I$, and let $f:\widehat Y\simeq\widehat Y$ be deduced from $j:U\subset Y$ by passing to the completions, so \inserted{$f(U)=\widehat Y-f(J)\subset Y=\widehat Y-I$} \deleted{\unsure{we have} $f(J)$)} implies $f(J)\supset I$ that is $J\supset f^{-1}(I)$.

We see that the passage from $H_1(U)$ to $H_1(Y)$, by filling in holes at $J\setminus I$, has as its kernel the \inserted{sum of the} $L_j$ with $j\in J\setminus I$, and for the analogous reason the kernel of $H_1(U)-H_1(Y)$ induced by $j$ is the sum of the $L_j$ with $j\in J\setminus f(I)$, moreover the $L_j$ $(j\in J)$ in $H_1(U)$ are $\simeq\widehat{\mathbb Z}$ (respectively $\widehat{\mathbb Z}'$ in characteristic $p>0$...) and the $\widehat{\mathbb Z}$-submodule generated is $\widehat{\mathbb Z}^{J}/\widehat{\mathbb Z}$ (diagonal), so \deleted{if $\operatorname{card}J\geq3$,} the submodules generated by two subsets \inserted{$I,I'$} of $J$ distinct from $J$, can be equal only if $I=I'$, or $I$ and $I'$ of cardinality $\mu-1$ (where $\mu=\operatorname{card}J$)
